==English==
{{was wotd|2008|March|22}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰeh₂-|id=shine}}
From {{inh|en|enm|epiphanie}}, from {{der|en|fro|epyphanie}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|epiphania}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἐπιφάνεια||manifestation, striking appearance}}, from {{m|grc|ἐπιφαίνω||I appear, display}}, from {{m|grc|ἐπι-||upon}} + {{m|grc|φαίνω||I shine, appear}}. English [[Epiphany]] (of [[Christ]]) since the 14th century, generic use since the 17th century.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈpɪfəni/|/ɪˈpɪfni/|/ɛˈpɪfəni/|a=RP,GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-epiphany.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-epiphany.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{hyph|en|epi|pha|ny}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} An [[illuminating]] [[realization]] or [[discovery]], often resulting in a personal feeling of [[elation]], [[awe]], or [[wonder]].
#: {{syn|en|aha moment|enlightenment|eureka moment|nirvana|satori}}
#: {{ux|en|It came to her in an '''epiphany''' what her life's work was to be.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Lipstick Traces|author=w:Greil Marcus|year_published=2009|year=1989|publisher=Faber & Faber|passage=Instead of examining institutions and classes, structures of economic production and social control, one had to think about “moments”—moments of love, hate, poetry, frustration, action, surrender, delight, humiliation, justice, cruelty, resignation, surprise, disgust, resentment, self-loathing, pity, fury, peace of mind—those tiny '''epiphanies''', Lefebvre said, in which the absolute possibilities and temporal limits of anyone's existence were revealed.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2021|artist={{w|Clairo}}|title={{w|Sling (album)|Amoeba}}|passage=Between the gaps, I was swimming laps / Got close to some '''epiphany''' / I'll convince a friend to join deep ends / Have your toes touch the lack of cement}}
#* {{RQ:New Yorker|url=https://www.newyorker.com/news/our-columnists/tony-hsieh-and-the-emptiness-of-the-tech-mogul-myth|title=Tony Hsieh and the Emptiness of the Tech-Mogul Myth|author=Jay Caspian Kang|date=May 9, 2023|passage=The logic of the standard biography—a formative event leads to an '''epiphany''' that creates the great man—doesn’t quite work when the greatness doesn’t have much to do with the man at all.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260921142827/https://www.newyorker.com/news/our-columnists/tony-hsieh-and-the-emptiness-of-the-tech-mogul-myth|archivedate=21 September 2026}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[manifestation]] or [[appearance]] of a [[divine]] or [[superhuman]] [[being]].
#: {{syn|en|theophany}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} {{alt case|en|Epiphany}}.

